findings.body:
`handleJSONSchemaChange` ends its success path with `return True`. `True` is not a TypeScript/JavaScript identifier (the literal is `true`), so `tsc` fails with "Cannot find name 'True'" and the web build/lint CI will fail.

At runtime it is worse than a typo: the `handlePayloadChange` call on the previous line still runs, the `ReferenceError` is thrown inside the `try`, and the `catch` swallows it and returns `null`. The feature appears to work only because of this accidental path, and any future change inside the `try` will be silently masked.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="130-142"}

::suggestion{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="137-140"}
    try {
      JSON.parse(value)
      handlePayloadChange('json_schema')(value)
      return true
    }
    catch {
      return null
    }

`handleJSONSchemaChange` refuses to write invalid JSON into `tempPayload`, so `tempPayload.json_schema` always holds the last *valid* text. Consequently the `jsonSchemaInvalid` toast in `handleConfirm` (line 320) can never trigger.

In practice: a user types a broken schema into the CodeEditor, the editor keeps showing the broken text, and clicking Save closes the modal and saves the previous valid schema (or none). No error is shown, so the user believes their edit was saved.

The fix is to either keep the raw editor text in local state and validate it on confirm, or surface the parse error inline next to the field and block Save while it is invalid.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="310-324"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The error toast exists but has no path to fire. Add a test that types invalid JSON and asserts Save is blocked or an error is shown; that test would currently fail.

`normalizedJsonSchema` is assigned in `handleConfirm` (line 251) but never read; it will trip `no-unused-vars` in lint. Separately, `isJsonSchemaEmpty` (line 237) repeats the same null/blank check that `handleJSONSchemaChange` already performs inline (line 132), so there are now three places deciding what "empty" means.

::code{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="237-258"}

One small helper used in both places, with the unused variable removed, would be clearer.

After this change `jsonObjectWrap` is no longer imported anywhere in the web app (`index.tsx` import was removed). Leaving an unused export in `config.ts` invites someone to reintroduce the wrapping behavior that this PR deliberately removed.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/config.ts" lines="1-7"}

Delete it, or keep it only if another consumer is planned.
